The wavefunction for a particle in a 1D box of length $L$ in the state $n=1$ is given by:
$$\psi_1(x) = \sqrt{\frac{2}{L}} \sin\left(\frac{\pi x}{L}\right)$$
The probability of finding the electron in the region $0 \leq x \leq \frac{1}{2}L$ is given by the integral of the square of the wavefunction over that region:
$$P = \int_0^{1/2 L} |\psi_1(x)|^2 dx = \int_0^{1/2 L} \frac{2}{L} \sin^2\left(\frac{\pi x}{L}\right) dx$$
We can use the identity $\sin^2(\theta) = \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{2}\cos(2\theta)$. So,
$$P = \frac{2}{L} \int_0^{1/2 L} \left(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{2}\cos\left(\frac{2\pi x}{L}\right)\right) dx$$
$$P = \frac{1}{L} \int_0^{1/2 L} \left(1 - \cos\left(\frac{2\pi x}{L}\right)\right) dx$$
$$P = \frac{1}{L} \left[x - \frac{L}{2\pi} \sin\left(\frac{2\pi x}{L}\right)\right]_0^{1/2 L}$$
$$P = \frac{1}{L} \left[\left(\frac{1}{2L} - \frac{L}{2\pi} \sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{2L}\right)\right) - (0 - \frac{L}{2\pi} \sin(0))\right]$$
