Since the process is adiabatic, we have $PV^\gamma = \text{constant}$. For a gas, $\gamma = \frac{C_p}{C_v}$. We are given $C_{V,m} = 28.8 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~K}^{-1} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. We need to find $C_p$ for CO2. $C_p = C_v + R$, where $R = 8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~K}^{-1} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. So, $C_p = 28.8 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~K}^{-1} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} + 8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~K}^{-1} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} = 37.114 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~K}^{-1} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$.
Then $\gamma = \frac{37.114}{28.8} \approx 1.29$.

